**BLOG POST BODY**

BLOG TITLE: Why Women With PCOS Should Stop Fasting for 16 Hours (and What to Do Instead)

\---

If you have PCOS and you have been told that intermittent fasting will help your insulin

resistance -- you were told something directionally correct but mechanistically incomplete.

The underlying principle is sound: reducing the frequency and duration of insulin elevation

during the day is one of the most direct tools available for improving insulin sensitivity.

Research confirms this.

The problem is not the goal. The problem is the specific protocol most women with PCOS

are given to achieve it.

THE PROBLEM WITH 16:8 FASTING FOR PCOS

The standard 16:8 fasting protocol requires the eating window to end at approximately

7-8pm and not restart until 11am-12pm the following day.

This means skipping breakfast.

In women with PCOS -- especially those with adrenal involvement or cortisol dysregulation --

the morning hours carry the highest cortisol output of the day.

This is called the Cortisol Awakening Response (CAR), a natural biological rhythm

that primes the body for activity and food intake.

When a woman with elevated baseline cortisol skips breakfast and enters the morning

in a fasted state, she is stacking two significant stressors:

high cortisol and low blood sugar simultaneously.

Cortisol raises blood glucose through gluconeogenesis.

The pancreas responds by releasing insulin.

But the cells are already insulin resistant.

The result is elevated insulin in the very window the protocol was designed to reduce it.

\[PERSONAL STORY PLACEHOLDER: Insert here.

Example: 'I tried 16:8 fasting for \[X weeks / months\].

I was skipping breakfast and trying to hold through until noon.

What I noticed was \[irritability / worse energy crashes / my period disappeared again /

I was eating more in my window and gaining weight\].

When I understood the cortisol mechanism, I understood exactly why.

My adrenal pattern meant morning fasting was one of the worst things I could do.'\]

THE 10-HOUR HORMONE OPTIMIZATION WINDOW

The 10-Hour Hormone Optimization Window works differently.

It is defined as the natural period between your last meal of the evening

and your first meal the following morning -- achieved primarily through overnight sleep.

During this window:

\- Insulin returns to baseline (the insulin clearance rate supports this in 10-12 hours

for most women, even with insulin resistance)

\- The liver shifts from glycogen storage to glucose regulation

\- Melatonin secretion proceeds without the suppressive effect of active digestion

\- The Cortisol Awakening Response prepares correctly for morning metabolic function

If you eat dinner at 7pm and breakfast at 7am, you have already achieved this window.

You are not fasting. You are sleeping.

The protocol does not require any new behaviour for most women --

it requires understanding the biological value of the window you already have

and protecting it from late-evening eating that disrupts it.

\[CLIENT RESULT PLACEHOLDER:

'\[Client first name\] was managing PCOS + pre-diabetes and had been told to fast 16 hours.

She couldn't sustain it and her cortisol markers were worsening.

We replaced the 16-hour protocol with the 10-Hour Hormone Optimization Window.

Within \[X weeks\], her fasting glucose \[dropped from X to Y\] / her morning energy improved /

her HbA1c moved from pre-diabetic range at her next blood draw.'\]

WHO THIS IS AND IS NOT FOR

This approach is for women with PCOS and insulin resistance or pre-diabetes

who want to support their metabolic health without meal skipping.

It is not appropriate for women with a history of disordered eating

for whom any meal-timing framework requires specialist support.

If this is you, please work with an eating disorder specialist alongside any protocol.

